{:edb/db-before-t 903 :edb/db-after-t 904 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534216 50 #inst "2026-10-11T17:53:19.421Z" 13194139534216 17592186045417 true] [17592186171710 1071 "![](images/1e/1ef5ddc89d7846d1126c60f34fc400669d23d6ca16c2b622addaf2916955ed27.svg)\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z) = z-y$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle u^2, \\: -v, \\: v \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v) \\in D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ where $u \\geq 0.$ What is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $\\| \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\| = 2\\sqrt{2}u.$*" 13194139534216 17592186045417 true] [17592186171710 1071 "![](images/6a/6ad2702c610c5e7d31d07584c6e605467ff25c8474befd433214451169cc1180.png)\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z) = z-y$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle u^2, \\: -v, \\: v \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v) \\in D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ where $u \\geq 0.$ What is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $\\| \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\| = 2\\sqrt{2}u.$*" 13194139534216 17592186045417 false] [17592186172130 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414460 13194139534216 17592186045417 true] [17592186172130 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414461 13194139534216 17592186045417 true] [17592186172131 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414462 13194139534216 17592186045417 true] [17592186172131 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414463 13194139534216 17592186045417 true] [17592186172188 1050 540.0 13194139534216 17592186045417 true] [17592186172377 1068 #edb/ref 17592186169125 13194139534216 17592186045417 false] [17592186172377 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414459 13194139534216 17592186045417 true] [17592186172377 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534216 17592186045417 true] [17592186172377 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral\n$∬_(S)(2x + y + z)dS$,\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by\n$r(u, v) = ⟨2u - v, 3u + 2, u + 2v⟩$\nover the domain $D = {(u, v): 0 \\le u \\le 1,-1 \\le v \\le 1}$.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖r_{u}^{′} \\cdot r_{v}^{′}‖ = \\sqrt{70}$.*" 13194139534216 17592186045417 false] [17592186172377 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}(2x+y+z)\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2u-v,\\,3u+2,\\,u+2v⟩\n$$\n\n \nover the domain $D={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,-1\\le v\\le 1}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{70}.$*" 13194139534216 17592186045417 true] [17592186172377 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{70}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(2x+y+z)\\,{|}_{x=2u-v\\,y=3u+2\\,z=u+2v} \\\\  & =2(2u-v)+(3u+2)+(u+2v) \\\\  & =8u+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(8u+2)\\sqrt{70}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{70}{∬}_{D}\\,(8u+2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{70}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(8u+2)\\,\\text{d}u\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\sqrt{70}\\cdot [4{u}^{2}+2u]_{0}^{1}\\cdot [u]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{70}\\cdot 6\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{70}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534216 17592186045417 true] [17592186173252 1064 "Consider the surface integral of the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = x^2+y^2+z^2\n$$\n\n \nover the parametric surface $S,$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle u, \\: 2v, \\: -u \\big\\rangle, \\quad (u,v)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is the unit square in the $uv$-plane given by\n\n$$\nD = \\{(u,v) \\: | \\: 0\\leq u\\leq 1,\\: 0\\leq v\\leq 1\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's compute the surface integral of $f$ over $S.$\n\nFor a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D,$ the surface integral of $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\|  \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the fundamental vector product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{'}\\times {r}_{v}^{'} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,0,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{'}\\times {r}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨2,\\,0,\\,2⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,{|}_{x=u\\,y=2v\\,z=-u} \\\\  & ={u}^{2}+(2v)^{2}+(-u)^{2} \\\\  & ={u}^{2}+4{v}^{2}+{u}^{2} \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}+4{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{'}\\times {r}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(2{u}^{2}+4{v}^{2})\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\,{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate this integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & =4\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\,{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\,{u}^{2}+4\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}2{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\,{u}^{2}+8\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v+8\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v+8\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534216 17592186045417 false] [17592186173252 1064 "Consider the surface integral of the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = x^2+y^2+z^2\n$$\n\n \nover the parametric surface $S,$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle u, \\: 2v, \\: -u \\big\\rangle, \\quad (u,v)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is the unit square in the $uv$-plane given by\n\n$$\nD = \\{(u,v) \\: | \\: 0\\leq u\\leq 1,\\: 0\\leq v\\leq 1\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's compute the surface integral of $f$ over $S.$\n\nFor a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D,$ the surface integral of $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\|  \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the fundamental vector product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,0,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨2,\\,0,\\,2⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,{|}_{x=u\\,y=2v\\,z=-u} \\\\  & ={u}^{2}+(2v)^{2}+(-u)^{2} \\\\  & ={u}^{2}+4{v}^{2}+{u}^{2} \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}+4{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(2{u}^{2}+4{v}^{2})\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\,{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate this integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & =4\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\,{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\,{u}^{2}+4\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}2{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\,{u}^{2}+8\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v+8\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v+8\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534216 17592186045417 true] [17592186173256 1064 "Consider a continuous scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ and a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ defined for $(u,v) \\in D,$ where $D\\subseteq \\mathbb R^2.$ \n\nIf our surface is \"sufficiently smooth,\" we can define the **surface integral** of $f$ over $S.$ Let's discuss how we might construct such an integral.\n\nFor the sake of simplicity, let's assume that our region $D$ is rectangular. We start by creating a partition $P$ of $D$ in the usual way:\n\n$$\nP = \\left\\{u_0, u_1, \\ldots, u_m\\right\\} \\times \\left\\{v_0, v_1, \\ldots, v_n\\right\\}\n$$\n\nOur partition is shown schematically below.\n\n![](images/6a/6a15c7b587503c82a57d0734c5bc5fda990824ad162724236868461cd92e4fbe.png)\n\n \n\nThe area $\\delta A = \\delta u\\delta v$ of the small rectangle highlighted in the diagram corresponds to a particular patch $S_{ij}$ of area $\\delta S_{ij}$ on our surface. \n\n![](images/1a/1a8774b7aa366d3328b6f81cfe7315b7784a1f5783f14c1f19f83a459cef5434.png)\n\n \n\nLet $P_{ij}^*$ be a point on the surface within the patch $S_{ij}.$ We consider the product \n\n$$\nf(P_{ij}^*) \\cdot \\delta S_{ij}.\n$$\n\nIf we compute this product for all patches and add them together, we get the Riemann sum\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n f(P_{ij}^*)\\cdot\\delta S_{ij}.\n$$\n\nTaking the limit as $\\delta u, \\delta v\\to 0$ gives our definition of the surface integral of $f$ over $S,$ which we denote as\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nFrom previous discussions, we know that the area of the small patch can be approximated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\delta S_{ij} \\approx \\| \\mathbf r'_u \\times \\mathbf r'_v\\|\\,\\delta u\\delta v = \\| \\mathbf r'_u \\times \\mathbf r'_v\\|\\,\\delta A\n$$\n\nIt follows that we can calculate our surface integral using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\|  \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nThis formula is also valid when $D$ is non-rectangular. \n\nLet's see an example of applying this in practice." 13194139534216 17592186045417 true] [17592186173256 1064 "Consider a continuous scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ and a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ defined for $(u,v) \\in D,$ where $D\\subseteq \\mathbb R^2.$ \n\nIf our surface is \"sufficiently smooth,\" we can define the **surface integral** of $f$ over $S.$ Let's discuss how we might construct such an integral.\n\nThe procedure is similar to what we've seen before (for example, regarding the line integrals over a curve). \n\nFor the sake of simplicity, let's assume that our region $D$ is rectangular. We start by creating a partition $P$ of $D$ in the usual way:\n\n$$\nP = \\left\\{u_0, u_1, \\ldots, u_m\\right\\} \\times \\left\\{v_0, v_1, \\ldots, v_n\\right\\}\n$$\n\nOur partition is shown schematically below.\n\n![](images/6a/6a15c7b587503c82a57d0734c5bc5fda990824ad162724236868461cd92e4fbe.png)\n\nThe area $\\delta A = \\delta u\\delta v$ of the small rectangle highlighted in the diagram corresponds to a particular patch $S_{ij}$ of area $\\delta S_{ij}$ on our surface. \n\n![](images/1a/1a8774b7aa366d3328b6f81cfe7315b7784a1f5783f14c1f19f83a459cef5434.png)\n\nLet $P_{ij}^*$ be a point on the surface within the patch $S_{ij}.$ We consider the product \n\n$$\nf(P_{ij}^*) \\cdot \\delta S_{ij}.\n$$\n\nIf we compute this product for all patches and add them together, we get the Riemann sum\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n f(P_{ij}^*)\\cdot\\delta S_{ij}.\n$$\n\nTaking the limit as $\\delta u, \\delta v\\to 0$ gives our definition of the surface integral of $f$ over $S,$ which we denote as\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nFrom previous discussions, we know that the area of the small patch can be approximated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\delta S_{ij} \\approx \\| \\mathbf r'_u \\times \\mathbf r'_v\\|\\,\\delta u\\delta v = \\| \\mathbf r'_u \\times \\mathbf r'_v\\|\\,\\delta A\n$$\n\nIt follows that we can calculate our surface integral using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\|  \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nThis formula is also valid when $D$ is non-rectangular. \n\nLet's see an example of applying this in practice." 13194139534216 17592186045417 false] [17592186414430 1080 "{u}^{2}(-{u}^{3}+{v}^{4})\\sqrt{4uv+u}" 13194139534216 17592186045417 true] [17592186414430 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534216 17592186045417 true] [17592186414430 1083 #uuid "0cd71aa2-2da0-53d3-a18e-57cc7dd66fe2" 13194139534216 17592186045417 true] [17592186414431 1080 "\\sqrt{70}" 13194139534216 17592186045417 true] [17592186414431 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534216 17592186045417 true] [17592186414431 1083 #uuid "11345332-1583-5403-b382-1f7db9544110" 13194139534216 17592186045417 true] [17592186414432 1080 "3\\sqrt{6}" 13194139534216 17592186045417 true] [17592186414432 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534216 17592186045417 true] [17592186414432 1083 #uuid "1b5899eb-77cf-5b34-b910-c93474d97e73" 13194139534216 17592186045417 true] [17592186414433 1080 "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" 13194139534216 17592186045417 true] [17592186414433 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534216 17592186045417 true] [17592186414433 1083 #uuid "22129875-cc14-5ad3-8bba-b26995c05188" 13194139534216 17592186045417 true] [17592186414434 1080 "{v}^{2}" 